<h1>Results</h1>
The output is one line per combination of storage format and restart ratio.
Run on Ginkgo's @p matrices/test/ani1.mtx with a cap of ten steps per cycle,
two restart ratios and three storage formats:

@code{.sh}
cb-gmres-sweep reference matrices/test/ani1.mtx 10 0,0.5 keep,reduce1,ireduce1
@endcode

@code{.cpp}
Matrix:      matrices/test/ani1.mtx  (36 x 36, 208 nonzeros)
krylov_dim:  10
restart_ratio: 0,0.5
solver:      CbGmres
precond:     none
rhs:         sinus
tolerance:   1e-10 (relative to |b|)

storage   restart_ratio   iters      time [s]     rel. res.
----------------------------------------------------------
keep      0                 54    3.8557e-04    5.8360e-11
keep      0.5               69    4.8053e-04    5.0478e-11
reduce1   0                 54    2.5378e-04    5.8359e-11
reduce1   0.5               69    4.7527e-04    5.0474e-11
ireduce1  0                 54    2.5720e-04    5.8360e-11
ireduce1  0.5               69    4.7383e-04    5.0478e-11
@endcode

<h3> Comments about programming and debugging </h3>
The iteration counts depend on the matrix and the storage format, and the times
on the executor and the machine. A positive restart ratio ends a cycle earlier
than the cap, which costs iterations on a problem this small and pays on the
large ones.
